# THE QIRAKI FILES — BOOK 5, “THE WEIGHT OF IT,” CHAPTER-BY-CHAPTER OUTLINE (v2)

**ID:** B5.OUT. **Tier:** 2, outline. **Owns:** chapter-level construction for all 47 chapters of Book 5. **Supersedes** Qiraki_Book5_Chapter_Outline (v1) entirely. Standing rules from Book 1’s outline front matter govern here identically.

**Depends on:** Reaction_Formula_Applied_Model_v6.docx, Qiraki_Book4_Chapter_Outline_v3.docx, claude_Qiraki_Matchset_Affection_Reference_v1.md, Qiraki_Character_Sheets_v16.docx, Qiraki_Combat_Curriculum_Reference_RECOVERED_v4.docx, Qiraki_Rune_Tech_Reference_v2.docx, Qiraki_Engineering_Curriculum_Reference_v5.docx, Qiraki_Points_Shop_Catalog.docx, Qiraki_Propaganda_Bank_RECOVERED.docx, Qiraki_Master_Style_Guide_v3.docx, Qiraki_Emotion_Craft_Reference_md.docx.

**Version:** v2, 2026-09-02.

**Note on v1.** v1 was new construction assembled from scattered locked material and it is the strongest document in the Books 2-6 tier. **Everything it decided is carried forward.** The convergence, Toma’s revision to talented rather than damaged, the Corw control case, the reposition’s framing as correction, Kest’s third non-registration, the July calendar correction, and every LOCKED item are unchanged. v2 adds the engine fields, resolves the four remaining PROPOSED items as **[CLAUDE]**, and expands thin chapters for drafting.

**Defect found in v1 and corrected here.** The Toma Ruiz material appears twice, once in a superseded washout-era form and once revised, with the superseded version left in place below the revision. **The revised version is authoritative. The duplicate is deleted in this file.** Worth a Defect Queue entry.

**Cadets are 16-17 this book.**

**Locked spine, unchanged:** combat weight increases hard, Cradle Circle interference turns visibly unjust, Ilyen repositions from Reeps to Munti.

## PART ZERO — DRAFTING HANDOFF

**Read this first if you are drafting from this file.**

**Books 1 through 4 must be drafted first.** Ch. 1’s opening is E→c from B4.CH47, and the reposition’s unstated origin is B4.CH31. **Read B4.CH25, B4.CH31 and B4.CH33 before drafting anything here.**

**Load order.** Chapter Build Protocol’s LOAD stage governs. In addition to the standing load, this book requires: B4.OUT in full, the Affection Reference, the Points Shop Catalog (the Quickfix and Lifebox tiers are load-bearing at Ch. 31), the Character Sheets entries for Ilyen Pral, Petra Lindqvist, Toma Ruiz and Kest, the Year 5 entries in all three curriculum references, and the Propaganda Bank’s Tier 3 month themes plus the four Year 5 curriculum banks.

**The five chapters that carry the book.** Ch. 21 (the suggestion, and the book’s loop), Ch. 29 (what precision is for, and Toma), Ch. 31 (the cost of switching), Ch. 42 (the reposition), Ch. 43 (what didn’t change).

**The single most important craft instruction in this file, carried from v1 and worth restating.** This is a book whose entire spine is institutional injustice, containing a sympathetic authority figure conducting a fairness review. **It is the most dangerous chapter set in the series for Rule 4.** The temptation is to have Kest almost see it, or to have a cadet nearly articulate it and stop. **Both are softer versions of stating it.** The discipline holds only if the reader assembles the contradiction with no character having gestured at the assembly. Watch Halvorsen and Osei specifically.

**The eight things this book will break if you are not watching for them.**

- **Stating the critique.** Above. Applies at maximum in Ch. 18, 25, 26 and 43.

- **Connecting Trav to Ilyen’s reposition.** §1.3. He is a reason she is in that room and nobody, including her, ever says so.

- **Making the fourth tell visible to Trav.** LOCKED. He reads it as her being good at her new role. **She is.** The tell only works because the innocent reading is also true.

- **Making the reposition read as a demotion or a rescue.** LOCKED as a correction. The institution does not fix her. Petra suggests, Toma teaches, and she pays for it herself at Ch. 31.

- **Letting Trav notice the autonomous-stage shift.** Ch. 13. His hard constraint forbids it, so the whole beat lives in what his body does and in someone else’s reaction.

- **Giving Flow Drills only to Trav.** Ch. 4. Automaticity is a documented outcome of four years of structured practice. If only the protagonist gets it, the science quietly turns back into talent.

- **Explaining the pain-spike.** Ch. 9. Third book running. Ironmind is military-era gear and there is no version of Book 5 where this is solved.

- **Naming interior emotion and the peak-line roughness rule.** Standing. Ch. 42’s peak is most at risk, because a genuinely joyful beat invites a well-made closing sentence.

**Word-count floor is 4,500.** Under it, add found-family socialization, not description.

**POV.** Trav throughout, except Ch. 10, which needs a wider view of Vrassik’s cohort. Ch. 21 is the book’s hardest POV problem: it is Ilyen and Petra’s scene and Trav is not in it. **[CLAUDE, recommendation:** take the wider view for Ch. 21. It is the book’s loop and running it secondhand through Trav would cost the book its best beat. Two POV exceptions in a book is within the locked discipline.**]**

## PART ONE — THE ENGINE

### 1.1 What replaced the emotional-target field

v1 carried a locked emotional target: frown dominant, real earned anger held all year, balanced by a smile once the reposition lands, with a comedy beat placed mid-book so it does not run entirely on grimness.

**Kept, unusually, and here is why.** Every other book in the tier carried a target that pre-selected D beat by beat, which is the inversion the audit condemned. **This one does not do that.** It names a register the book holds and a structural requirement that the register be broken once. That is a constraint on tone, not a pre-decided reaction, and the comedy beat’s placement in Act 1 is a genuine craft call. **It stays.** The two engine fields below are added alongside it rather than replacing it.

### 1.2 c comes from E, and Book 5 opens in a room he has no business in

**[FORMULA, E→c, inherited from B4.OUT §Ch. 47, proposed override of v1’s Ch. 1.]**

**What Book 4 left.** In Trav: a half-learned Munti discipline he has no path to, months old, unabandoned, started because a boy died forty metres from him and the extraction was late. In Reqa: a reflex that finally has somewhere to go and four months of practice at it. In Denic: hours spent teaching someone a job for reasons neither of them said. In Ilyen: a filed observation and two unfinished sentences.

**So Book 5 opens in the Munti room.** A full year on. Trav still turning up, still not good at it, and **Ilyen in the doorway.** She has been coming to watch for a while. Neither of them says why either of them is there.

**Then the year.** The combat load visibly up, mechanically rather than by announcement, exactly as v1 has it. Bigger draw, more heat, gear that behaves differently late in a session than early. **The override changes the chapter’s first movement and nothing else.**

### 1.3 Trav’s Gate 1, and the year his chain finally does something

**[FORMULA, and this is the payoff of four books.]**

The chain: Book 1 he grinds the arena. Book 2 he re-aims onto whoever is worst. Book 3 he specifies into standing near people and it nearly kills him. Book 4 someone dies anyway, so he goes to learn the thing that failed, and he cannot be a Munti and never will be.

**Book 5 is the year it works, and he never finds out.**

He keeps going to the Munti room. He is still bad at it. The only outcome of a full year of a Meeps turning up to a discipline he has no path to is that **a girl with a four-year failure behind her watched somebody go be bad at something on purpose, for months, and eventually walked in.**

**[FORMULA, Gate 7, propagation through an absent archetype.]** The formula’s own case: the effect lands somewhere the actor never sees. **Trav is not the reason Ilyen repositions. He is one reason, unstated, uncredited, and unavailable to him.** Petra suggests it. Toma teaches it. Her own precision earns it. **And she had been standing in a doorway for a year first.**

**No character states this. Ilyen does not state it, including to herself.** Her four excellent reasons at Ch. 42 are all true and this is not among them.

**The year’s want-hits, and what he does.** The interference at Ch. 25 does not touch his want and he idles through it, live, which is why the book’s anger is the cohort’s rather than his. **What touches his want is Ch. 9’s Ilyen chapter**, a person he cannot help, sitting in a settled failure. His action is the same action it has been for three years: he shows up. It is the least dramatic reaction in the series and it is the fourth turn of the same chain.

**The cost, and it is real.** At Ch. 31 she goes backward in public, four tiers below a peer, in front of people who watched her struggle for four years. **He is part of why she is in a position to pay that.** Mis-aimed again, in the direction of a good outcome, which is the only version of mis-aimed this chain has not produced yet.

### 1.4 Trav’s Matter, tracked, and this is the book’s strangest column

| Chapter | Matter | What moved it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 4 | rising | Flow Drills. Bandwidth freed. Capability arriving without effort for the first time. |
| 13 | **peak, physical** | Fluid. The autonomous stage. His body is now the most capable instrument he has ever had and he did not notice it happen. |
| 18, 25, 26 | **floor, institutional** | Interference. He can see a pattern, he is right about it, and he has no mechanism of any kind. |
| 42 | **not his** | The largest agency event in the book belongs to somebody else and he contributed to it invisibly. |

**The two columns run opposite directions in the same year and no character observes that.** Physically he has never been more able. Institutionally he has never been more powerless. **That is the book, and it is structural rather than thematic, and it stays structural.**

### 1.5 Collisions and the loop

- **Act 1, Ch. 6.** The event that got away from everyone. **A collision with ten participants and no antagonist**, which is what an escalation actually is.

- **Act 2, Ch. 21.** **The book’s loop.** Petra and Ilyen. Three full passes. Reserved for the relationship the book is actually about, which this year is not Trav’s. See there.

- **Act 3, Ch. 29.** Toma’s D lands on Ilyen and the reason behind it never reaches her. **A one-directional collision where the a is received and the cause is permanently unavailable.**

- **Act 3, Ch. 32.** Corw’s D lands on the team as friction. Real, unglamorous, resolved by rupture and repair rather than by anyone being generous.

### 1.6 Matchset material, per the Affection Reference

**Ilyen’s fourth tell is technique 3 at its purest and is the flagship example of the whole document.** Deniable competence: a good Munti stocking for their team’s actual consumption is not a favor, it is the job. **The innocent reading is entirely true.** That is what makes it work and it is why Ch. 32’s control case is structurally required.

**Trav/Reqa, Ch. 15.** Technique 3 running in both directions at once. He never asks what the Heirloom project is for. She never says. **Neither of them experiences that as concealment.**

**Petra/Ilyen, Ch. 21 and Ch. 35.** The book’s primary matchset and the loop. Six years of holding something out of plain loyalty.

**Corw/Fenn, Ch. 11.** The arc resolving. **Denic/Doyle, Ch. 20.**

**Secondary matchsets carry forward**, one instance each, unremarked: Talia and Sana’s grip tape, Bruvald and Vekk’s flat hand, Jifsook and Reya’s early trash-talk, Lissrak and Faela’s number read off a chassis. **Ch. 28 and Ch. 45 are the natural homes.**

### 1.7 The unspendable register

- **Why Ilyen walked into that room.** §1.3.

- **Nobody else’s consumables are stocked that far ahead.** Ch. 32 is the control and Ch. 44 is the tell and no line connects them.

- **The machines got heavier in the year the pilots got lighter.** The convergence, carried from v1. Structural, never stated.

- **Toma’s reason.** Ch. 29. He does not mention Soren. Ilyen has no reason to connect it. **Trav was in that arena and says nothing**, which is exactly what his cognition does.

- **Incursion frequency has never once decreased.** Ch. 33. Sold as proof the line works. Also proof the thing behind it has never needed to try harder. **Never resolved in narration.**

## ACT ONE — The year gets heavier, Ch. 1-12, January into March

**Governing psychology:** abstract reasoning genuinely online for the first time, which is exactly what makes this the year institutional unfairness becomes visible. Younger cadets experienced Book 2’s legacy gear and Book 3’s bad path choice as discrete bad events. Sixteen-year-olds can hold a pattern in mind and recognize a system, and that recognition is the engine of the year’s anger. **Real developmental fact, not narrative convenience.**

### Ch. 1 — Heavier

**Scene-want, Ilyen:** to keep standing in the doorway without having to explain it. **Scene-want, Trav:** to get the drill right, and he will not.

**[CLAUDE, the §1.2 override, first movement.]** The Munti room. Twelve months on. He is a Meeps in a repair-and-diagnostic session, welcome enough, useless enough, and it is completely unremarkable to everyone present because it has been going on for a year. **Ilyen is in the doorway and does not come in.**

**Then the year.** Fifth January, combat load visibly up, mechanical rather than announced. Bigger draw, more heat, gear that behaves differently late in a session than early. Discipline month’s Tier 3 fragment against a cohort that genuinely does not need discipline explained.

*Psych: sixteen-year-olds notice load increases as an insult before they notice them as a curriculum.*

### Ch. 2 — Circles

**Scene-want, Ilyen:** to be the best in the room, which she still is and which still does not translate.

Year 5 rune-patterning. Closed self-referencing loops, real control theory, a construct that checks its own output and adjusts without being told to run again. **The most conceptually satisfying material the cadets have met yet and it should be genuinely satisfying to read.**

**[CLAUDE, curriculum]** Build the ladder visibly: Year 2 was Sequence, ordering. Year 3 was Knots, boolean. Year 4 was Branching, conditional. **Year 5 closes the loop and the construct becomes something that corrects itself**, which is the first material in five years that does something a cadet could not do faster by hand.

**Fragment placement, and this is the single best-placed line in the book.** The Year 5 rune-tech bank’s overheard line about the legacy track not getting this material until later, if at all, draw your own conclusions. **Circulated among scholarship-track cadets, never issued by anyone.** It goes here, at the top, cold, in a chapter whose own content is a cadet discovering how capable the material actually is. **Nothing in the chapter acknowledges it.**

### Ch. 3 — Heat

**Scene-want, Reqa:** to get to the part where it fails.

Year 5 engineering. Thermodynamics. Power in means heat out and there is no version of the system that avoids it. Cooling as a real design constraint rather than an afterthought.

**This chapter is the mechanism behind Ch. 1** and should follow closely enough that a reader connects heavier gear to hotter gear without either being told to.

**[CLAUDE, curriculum, and this is the convergence’s load-bearing half.]** The teachable point is that efficiency is not a percentage you improve toward a hundred, it is a hard ceiling with physics behind it, and every watt you do not convert to motion you have to put somewhere. **A more capable machine is a hotter machine and that is not an engineering failure, it is the deal.**

### Ch. 4 — Flow Drills

**Scene-want, Vantree:** to not help them, and it costs her.

New combat coursework. Reduced cueing, high repetition, sequences drilled until they stop requiring conscious attention. **Deliberately unglamorous, a montage-shaped chapter that should refuse to be a montage.**

**Craft note, carried from v1 and it matters.** The drill works on the whole cohort, not on Trav specifically. Autonomous-stage automaticity is a documented outcome of four years of structured practice, and if only the protagonist gets it the science quietly turns back into a talent.

**Vantree beat.** Reduced cueing means stepping back and letting them fail unhelped, which is the opposite of everything protective in her. **She does it anyway.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Fifth year of her in the background and this is her largest beat since B1.CH27. Her locked register is that brutal preparation is the actual act of care, and this is the chapter where the two halves of that are visibly in tension inside one person for the first time. **She says nothing about it. The tension is in how long she waits before speaking.**

### Ch. 5 — What They Don’t Get

**Scene-want, three different cadets, and that is the structure.**

First hard Cradle Circle beat of the year. Resource allocation quietly favoring legacy-track cadets, escalating the seed planted in B2.CH07 and paid off in B2.CH34. **Nobody says the word unfair.**

**Structural note, carried from v1.** This needs to be a *pattern* rather than an incident, because the whole developmental point of the year is that these kids can now see patterns. **One event repeated across three cadets in one chapter does what three separate chapters would.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Write the same shape three times with three different cadets and let the third one be the moment the reader clicks. **No character clicks on the page.** One of the three should be a legacy-track cadet who benefits and is mildly embarrassed and gets over it, per B2.CH34’s likeable-beneficiary principle.

### Ch. 6 — The Event That Got Away From Everyone

**The book’s locked comedy beat, placed here on purpose.**

Trope draw: a monthly social event escalating well past what anyone intended. **[CLAUDE, ruling on the vehicle]** A Discipline-month training-ramp tradition, which is the January-adjacent option and keeps Ambition and Unity free for their own chapters.

**Escalation beat, joke-ritual arc.** Trav’s humor is well developed by sixteen and doing real social work, four books past the ritual’s origin and one book past its reversal.

**[FORMULA, Gate 6, a collision with ten participants and no antagonist.]** Nobody escalates it. **Each person’s reasonable response to the previous person’s reasonable response is one notch further out**, which is what an escalation actually is and is much funnier than anyone deciding to cause trouble.

*Psych: this age group’s capacity to turn a sanctioned activity into something else entirely is not rebellion, it is ordinary, and the institution’s reaction is more interesting than the escalation.*

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** The institution’s reaction is the chapter’s second half and should be neither punitive nor indulgent. **Somebody in administration handles it correctly, proportionately, and with visible experience**, because this happens every year and they know it does. **That is funnier and bleaker than a crackdown.**

### Ch. 7 — Study Session I

**Scene-want, Reqa:** to point out that her side got there first.

**Subject: feedback loops**, the Circles material read against what a thermostat or an autopilot actually does. Genuinely real control theory, no invention required.

**[MATCH]** Her engineering preference over rune-patterning is locked, and this is a subject where her side and his side describe the same phenomenon in different vocabularies. **Worth letting her enjoy openly.** Five books of sessions and this is the first one she wins outright on subject matter.

### Ch. 8 — Ilyen, Four Years In

**Scene-want, Ilyen:** nothing. That is the chapter.

Her Reeps problem is no longer a struggle, it is a settled fact, and that is worse. Book 3 showed it starting, Book 4 showed it compounding, this shows it as a thing everyone including her has stopped expecting to change. **No softening.**

*Psych: at sixteen, a four-year failure has stopped being a setback and started being an identity, which is precisely why the correction later has to be shown as a correction rather than a rescue.*

**[FORMULA, and this is the year’s want-hit for Trav.]** A person he cannot help, in a failure that has stopped moving. Gate 1 fires. **His action is that he shows up**, which is the same action as the last three years and by now is so ordinary that neither the prose nor any character marks it.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** The hardest thing about this chapter is that it has no event. Write the absence of struggle. She is not trying harder. She has stopped having bad days about it, which reads as improvement for about two pages and then does not.

### Ch. 9 — The Pain, Still

Symbiosis spike carries over from Books 3 and 4. Unresolved, still unexamined. **Hard constraint unchanged: never internally explained, only shown.**

**Timing note.** Ironmind is B-tier military-era gear and arrives long after these years rather than fixing them retroactively. **There is no version of Book 5 where this gets solved.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Fourth book of this and the escalation must be measurable against B3.CH45 and B4.CH02. Short chapter. **He has now had it long enough that he has stopped noticing he has it**, which is a new and worse stage and is shown by the absence of the small physical adjustments the earlier books gave him.

### Ch. 10 — Vrassik, Elsewhere

**POV exception, wider view.**

Vrassik rising through his own cohort’s version of the harder year, mostly off Trav’s page per his locked arc. Post-ritual confidence from Book 4 compounding. Jifsook, Lissrak, Bruvald, Krethis and Perrin all present.

Good chapter for an Osnius fragment against Bruvald, and for Lissrak’s devotion to keep being legible only in retrospect.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Krethis’s crest tell has fired in five books and is still unexplained. **Sixth instance here.** By now a returning reader is actively waiting on it and a first-time reader has stopped seeing it, and both of those are correct.

### Ch. 11 — Corw, Arriving

Corw’s Tank identity turning the corner it started turning in Book 3, the ego-test arc resolving into something useful. **The path itself was always the test**, a top-ranked cadet on the one path whose success metric is how well the team did rather than how well he did.

**[MATCH, Fenn.]** Her dry non-reaction still doing quiet work five books on. **[CLAUDE]** At B4.CH21 it stopped working and she noticed. This is the chapter where it works again, and the reason is not that she changed anything. **He arrived.** She does not get credit and does not want any and does not know she is owed some.

### Ch. 12 — The Choice

Weekend-choice chapter. **No propaganda-fragment opener**, per the weekend exception.

Home is now genuinely strange to kids this far in, and for several of them this is one of the last few.

*Psych: at sixteen the loyalty conflict has stopped being painful and started being simply true, which reads sadder than the Book 1 version.*

**Yssa beat. First visit after Soren. He does not tell her.** **Not concealment as a decision, concealment as the absence of one**, since he does not process backward and there is nothing in him that would produce the sentence. **She notices something and does not get it out of him.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Fifth Yssa appearance and the fourth to end on her asking whether he is eating. **This one she does not ask.** Nothing marks the omission. **B7.CH38 runs the scene again and the reader should have four instances behind them by then.**

## ACT TWO — What the body learned and what the institution didn’t, Ch. 13-23, April into July

**Governing psychology:** the autonomous stage arriving is genuinely pleasurable, competence stops costing attention, and that pleasure sits directly against a year of institutional grinding. **Both are real at once and neither resolves the other.**

### Ch. 13 — Fluid

**Trav’s locked fluid-and-evasive shift lands here.**

Not a training breakthrough and not a montage payoff. The textbook description of a real athlete reaching the autonomous stage on exactly the timeline four years of structured escalating practice would predict.

**He does not notice it happening. Someone else does.**

**Craft note.** Per his hard constraint he cannot register this internally, so the entire beat has to live in what his body does and in another character’s reaction to it.

**[CLAUDE, ruling on who notices.]** Vrassik. Not a teammate, not an instructor, not Reqa. **He is the only person who has kept a five-year baseline on Trav specifically** and he has been wrong about him twice already, at B3.CH22 and B3.CH25. **This time he is right, and he says it plainly, and he is not pleased about it.**

**[FORMULA, Gate 2, Matter peak.]** His body is now the most capable instrument he has ever had. **Ch. 18 is five chapters later and it is the floor of the other column.**

### Ch. 14 — Cooling

Engineering’s thermodynamics coursework meets the heavier gear directly. A real design exercise where the heat has nowhere to go.

**[CLAUDE, accepting v1’s PROPOSED fill.]** The Year 5 cooling component is the **Bleedline**, a passive thermal-shunt assembly, the cheap standard-issue answer that works fine until the draw increases, which is exactly what happened in Ch. 1. Kid-slang naming per the standing convention.

**[CLAUDE]** B3.CH14’s equipment failure was human error at fourteen-year-old reliability. **This is a category rhyme:** the cheap standard answer that is genuinely adequate right up until the conditions change. **Neither book points at the other.**

### Ch. 15 — Study Session II

**Subject: thermal management**, why every watt you do not account for becomes a problem you meet later and usually at the worst time. Real, teachable, directly load-bearing on the year’s combat weight.

**Heirloom project, fourth beat, and Year 5’s curriculum is the wall she hits.** A weapon that creates and manipulates circles in real time draws continuously rather than in bursts, which under this year’s thermodynamics means it produces heat continuously and has nowhere to put it. **The problem is not that her design is wrong. The problem is that it works and cooks itself.**

That is a genuinely harder and more interesting obstacle than a design flaw, and it comes free from curriculum that was locked before this thread existed. **She does not solve it here.**

**[MATCH, technique 3, both directions at once.]** Trav is present, does not understand the scale of what she is attempting, and helps anyway by being someone to think out loud at. **He never asks what it is for. She never says.** Neither of them experiences that as concealment.

### Ch. 16 — The Rule, Enforced

**Trope draw, LOCKED for this book.** A rule everyone breaks that suddenly gets enforced on one specific cadet. Strong structural fit for exactly this year’s kind of unfairness.

**[CLAUDE, accepting v1’s PROPOSED resolution.]** It does not get fixed. The cadet absorbs it. **The only thing that changes is what the cohort now knows about how enforcement actually works.** That is the version that serves the register and the version that hurts.

**[CLAUDE]** B2.CH25 drew the same trope four years earlier and resolved it the same way. **The difference is that at thirteen they experienced it as a bad thing happening to someone, and at sixteen they can hold the pattern.** Neither book references the other. **The rhyme is the point and it only works if it is silent.**

### Ch. 17 — The Legacy Track

The resource gap made structural rather than anecdotal. The rune-tech curriculum is the honest mechanism, per the reference doc’s own Year 5 note that this is the year cadets start noticing how much more capable their material is than the legacy track’s coursework often lets on.

**Worth the inversion, carried from v1:** the scholarship track is getting the *better* education here. **That is a genuinely more interesting and more damning shape than the legacy kids simply getting more.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting, and this is where Rule 4 is hardest in the book.]** The chapter contains the whole indictment and no one may assemble it. **The technique is to give the reader two facts in different registers and no connective tissue:** a scholarship cadet doing something with Circles that is genuinely impressive, and a legacy cadet’s coursework described neutrally in a completely different scene. **Do not put them in the same conversation.**

### Ch. 18 — Talia, Steady

**Scene-want, Talia:** to know whether the thing she has been doing for five years was the right thing.

Her grind still working, and this is the year it starts visibly outpacing people who were ahead of her at thirteen. Her friction with Trav has been honest rather than petty for four books and should now be nearly spent, **setting up her Book 6 decision to opt out of the transfer race knowing herself well enough to choose the path that suits her.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** B2.CH44 is where she stopped wanting him to understand. B4.CH43 is where she found out that stopping did not make the wanting go away. **This is where it finishes going away, and the way it finishes is that she gets an answer from the work rather than from him.** He is barely in the chapter.

### Ch. 19 — June

Ensemble day. Curiosity month’s Tier 3 fragment. **First Year 5 Bloom fragment lands**, the Osnian oral-tradition line about it not hating us, hate meaning it noticed us at all, what it does being closer to how grass does not hate a field.

**Placement note, carried from v1:** this is the single best Bloom fragment in the bank and it should land in the calmest chapter available, not the tensest.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Word-count floor chapter. Ten kids. **The fragment goes at the top and then nothing for four thousand words**, and the four thousand words should be as warm as anything in the book.

### Ch. 20 — Denic, Five Books In

Denic’s Munti work fully load-bearing, the loud kid nobody pegged for it now the reason the team functions. **Placed deliberately ahead of Ilyen’s reposition so the reader already understands what the Munti role actually is before she chooses it.**

**Craft note.** His tell is cockpit stillness, hands loud everywhere else and exact in there. **Five books of that and no one has ever remarked on it, which is the point.**

**[MATCH, Doyle.]** **[CLAUDE]** Doyle has been arranging benches around Denic’s unstated aptitude since Book 2. This year Denic is the team’s whole margin and Doyle’s careful numbers are what he stands on, and neither has said any of it in five years. **One line. It should not be a moment.**

**[CLAUDE, and this is the chapter that makes Ch. 31 hurt.]** The reader finishes this chapter understanding that being a good Munti takes years. **Eleven chapters later Ilyen starts at the bottom of it at sixteen.**

### Ch. 21 — The Suggestion

**POV exception, wider view. The book’s loop.**

Somebody first raises the idea that Ilyen should reposition.

**[CLAUDE, resolving v1’s fork, and taking v1’s own recommendation.]** **Petra.** Her mek, the person she trusts with things she does not say elsewhere, seeded in Book 1. **The Guardian would make it institutional and cold. An instructor would make it authoritative and slightly humiliating.** Petra protects the reposition from reading as the institution finally getting something right, in a book whose entire spine is the institution getting things wrong.

**[FORMULA, Gate 6, three full passes, reserved for the relationship the book is actually about.]**

**Pass one.** Petra’s a is six years of accumulated watching. She has clocked Ilyen’s feelings since Book 2 and has held it out of plain loyalty, and she has also watched a friend fail at one thing for four years. **Her D is that she says the smaller of the two things.** She suggests the reposition. **She does not say the other thing and this is the fourth book in which she does not.**

**Pass two.** It lands on Ilyen and what lands is not the suggestion, it is that Petra has been paying that much attention. **Her D is defensive and it is not aimed at Petra**, it is aimed at the idea, and it comes out as a technical objection about rank and points and equipment tiers, all of which are correct, and all of which she has already worked out for herself. **The fact that she has the numbers ready is the tell that she has been thinking about it.**

**Pass three.** It lands back on Petra. **She hears the prepared numbers and understands what they mean and does not say that either.** She agrees with the objection. She says the numbers are right and drops it.

**[MATCH]** Technique 3. **Petra wins by conceding**, and the concession is real rather than tactical, and Ilyen decides on her own three chapters later without either of them referring back to this.

**Nothing about the crush is on the page in this chapter. Nothing is stated. Petra’s six-year silence is the loudest thing in the book and it is entirely made of omissions.**

### Ch. 22 — Gratitude

**July’s actual locked theme, correcting a calendar defect v1 introduced and fixed.**

**And the Tier 3 fragment for it is the best-placed line available anywhere in this book:** some of the ones you would thank are not here to hear it, say it to the ones who are.

**That lands roughly one year after Soren died, in a book where the cohort’s anger has nowhere to go, and it is not commented on by anyone.**

Ensemble chapter otherwise. Rivalry energy carrying forward, Vrassik and Trav both climbing and still mostly apart this year.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** The fragment is institutional voice and is therefore obscene in context, and nobody in the cohort finds it obscene, because they have been reading this material for five years and it has worn through. **That is the horror and no line points at it.**

### Ch. 23 — Midpoint

Act 2 closer, ensemble, every relationship gets a real beat. **The team knows Ilyen is considering something and nobody has said the word out loud.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Word-count floor chapter, Gate 0 across all ten. **The not-saying is the chapter’s whole social texture:** ten people carefully talking around one subject for four thousand words, badly, and everyone knowing everyone is doing it, and it being affectionate rather than awkward.

## ACT THREE — Interference, Ch. 24-36, August into October

**Governing psychology:** the year’s anger reaching the point where it has somewhere to go, and the specific adolescent experience of being certain you are right about an injustice and having no mechanism whatsoever to act on it.

### Ch. 24 — Ambition Month

August, sharpest rivalry energy of the year, and the ordinary year continuing at full intensity right alongside the institutional thread.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Fifth Ambition month. **The theme months have been worn through since Book 3 and the fragments are Tier 3 now, which is the propaganda’s own admission that it has stopped trying.** The cohort does not notice, because you do not notice the thing that has always been getting quieter.

### Ch. 25 — Interference

**The year’s locked big beat, and it has to be undeniable rather than suspected.**

Cradle Circle interference peaks, affecting more than just Ilyen, visible enough that the cohort can name the pattern even if they cannot name a person responsible. **The escalation of everything seeded since Book 2.**

*Psych: black-and-white fairness judgment has matured into pattern recognition by this age, and pattern recognition without recourse is the actual emotional content of the chapter.*

**[FORMULA]** Gate 1: it does not touch Trav’s want and he idles through it, live. **The anger in this book is the cohort’s, not his**, and that is worth protecting, because a protagonist who carries the political register would flatten Book 5 into a lesson.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** The undeniability is structural. **Write it so that a reader who tried to argue the other side could not.** Then have no character be able to do anything, and end the chapter on something else entirely.

### Ch. 26 — Kest, The Third Time

**LOCKED. Unchanged from v1 and I would not touch a word.**

Kest is at the academy for a genuine policy review of enrollment fairness across his region, the kind of visit that happens on its own institutional cycle regardless of who is enrolled. Ilyen’s case is one example he addresses within that review. **Not personal targeting and not a dedicated trip.**

He is warm, certain, sincere, and completely convinced, the same register as Books 1 and 4 and the same he carries in Book 6. **He addresses a real unfairness genuinely and the review changes nothing structural, and both are true at once without the chapter reconciling them.**

**Consistency requirement, carried from B4.CH27 and setting up B6.CH42:** Trav is present and does not register or retain who this is. **Third non-registration, same consistent cognitive logic each time**, not repetition. An administrator addressing someone else’s problem is exactly the kind of thing his RPD-style attention does not snag on.

**Stale-doc flag, carried from v1:** Qiraki_Book_Expansion_Plan still lists this as an open question. The character sheet resolved it. **The Expansion Plan should be corrected rather than reconciled.**

### Ch. 27 — Study Session III

**[CLAUDE, accepting v1’s PROPOSED subject.]** The Structural Read revisited at Year 5 capability, specifically what changes when a schematic can be read by a construct that adjusts to what it finds rather than by a cadet reading a fixed output.

**Deliberately reaches back to B3.CH10 so the reader can measure two years of growth against a subject they already met**, which is a use the study-session device has never gotten yet.

**[MATCH]** B3.CH10 was Reqa walking his schematic across a building by hand because the transfer is manual and there is no other way. **She still does that. The construct does not change the walk.**

### Ch. 28 — Cross-Cohort

Trav’s team and Vrassik’s in shared exercise. Krethis’s six-year resentment still unresolved and still unexplained. Jifsook needling her brother. Lissrak steady. **Hiopi cultural fragment as the opener rather than anything Coalition-issued.**

**[MATCH]** Two secondary rituals get their instance here: Jifsook and Reya’s early trash-talk, Lissrak and Faela’s number off the chassis. **Neither near the other.**

### Ch. 29 — What Precision Is For

**The chapter that has to earn the reposition, not the reposition chapter itself.**

Ilyen’s first real exposure to Munti work, and the moment the reader sees why it fits. **Everything that made her the cohort’s rune-patterning prodigy and a bad Reeps** — precision, patience, the unprompted private log of every AI-tutor correction the dorm ever got — **is the exact profile the role wants.**

**Toma Ruiz, LOCKED placement, merged here, no new chapter.** He is the one who shows her the work. Same cohort, same year, two years into the path she is entering with nothing. **A peer rather than a mentor, which keeps the institution out of it.**

**He is good.** He is not a damaged kid clinging to a fixation, he is a genuinely excellent fifteen-year-old Munti, and the thing he is excellent at is **extraction timing specifically.** Not repair, not fabrication, not the parts that generate points. Cycle time, drilled past what anyone asked for, taught to her as if it were the only part of the job that counted.

**The reason is never stated and no character supplies it.** He does not mention Soren. Ilyen was not on that team and has no reason to connect it. **Trav is POV, was in that arena, and says nothing**, which is exactly what his cognition does with everything. **The reader has B4.CH25 and B4.CH31 and can assemble it alone.**

**Why the talented version is better than the washout version it replaced.** A kid who fails and then declines is the expected shape, and it makes his guilt legible *through* his decline, which is a soft way of telling the reader how to feel about him. **A kid who fails once and answers it by becoming the best in the cohort at the exact thing that failed is stranger, colder, and refuses to resolve into either tragedy or redemption.** It also makes Ilyen’s inheritance craft rather than damage.

**[CLAUDE, and this is the §1.4 doubling closing.]** B4.CH31 had Trav in a Munti room for the same reason, in the same weeks, in a different room. **Toma is now teaching Ilyen, who has been watching Trav do it for a year.** Three people, one failure, and none of the three lines are drawn. **Do not draw them.**

**[CLAUDE]** The first thing she learns about the role is that the part which matters most is the part nobody scores you on. **That is a fair description of her entire six-year characterization and is never stated as one.**

### Ch. 30 — Unity

September, cultural exchange, interspecies texture, runway toward Homecoming.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Fifth Unity. Word-count floor chapter. **The staleness is shown by writing it with the energy drained, never by anyone observing it.**

### Ch. 31 — The Cost of Switching

**This is what keeps the reposition from being free.**

Repositioning means starting the Munti equipment line near the bottom while her cohort is deep into their own paths. **Quickfix kit, physical dock required for every repair, no remote function at all**, after four years of accumulated Reeps investment. Real rank cost, real points cost, **real visible regression in front of people who watched her struggle for four years already.**

**One line of Toma here, no more.** He is already carrying a **Lifebox**, the cockpit-evacuation tier, bought earlier than most of the cohort managed. **Under the revision that reads as a good Munti prioritizing correctly rather than as penance, and it is deliberately impossible to tell which from the outside.** She is four tiers below it and the gap is the actual measure of what she has taken on. **Neither of them remarks on it.**

*Psych: at sixteen, voluntarily going backward in front of a peer group is close to the hardest available social act, and the fact that she does it is the actual characterization.*

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** Spend real words on the catalog. The reader should feel the tier gap as a number. **This is the book’s largest Matter event and it belongs to Ilyen, and it goes down before it goes up.**

### Ch. 32 — Corw and the Team

The unit absorbing a member changing roles mid-stream. Real friction, real adjustment, **not instant support.**

*Psych: rupture-and-repair, still how trust deepens, even at sixteen.*

**The control case, and Ch. 44’s tell does not work without it.** Corw waits on a part. Not a crisis, not a grievance, just a Tank with the highest consumption on the team sitting idle for a day because the thing he needs was not stocked ahead. **He grumbles, it gets sorted, the chapter moves on.**

**Placement is deliberate**, early enough that nobody connects the two, late enough to still be in memory.

**[FORMULA, Gate 6.]** Corw’s D is the friction and it is legitimate. **The team is genuinely worse for a while and nobody is being unkind.** The repair happens because time passes and people adjust, not because anyone is generous about it.

### Ch. 33 — Vigilance

October. Second and third Year 5 Bloom fragments, **including the access-logged internal briefing slide that incursion frequency has not decreased in sixty years and has never once decreased.**

The official framing sells that as proof the line works. **It is also, unstated, proof the thing behind the line has never once needed to try harder.**

**Never resolve that in narration.**

### Ch. 34 — Vrassik, Sharper

Rivalry intensifying into exam season. **Fourth blood, and notably less damaging than Books 3 and 4**, both of them past the associative stage and expressing skill as precision rather than as injury.

**Sets up B6.CH11 explicitly**, where the clean quality of their sparring is remarked on.

**[CLAUDE]** He said something plain at Ch. 13 and was not pleased about it. **This is the chapter where he does something about it, and what he does is get better, which is the only response he has ever had to anything.**

### Ch. 35 — The Cohort Holds

Ensemble chapter, every core relationship gets one more beat before Act 4.

**Mek beat. This book: Petra Lindqvist.** She clocked Ilyen’s feelings years ago and has never told anyone, out of plain loyalty rather than instruction. **In the book where Ilyen finally gets to be good at something in public, Petra is the only person who knows what that costs her and what it does not fix.**

**She says none of it.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** This is the payoff of Ch. 21 and it should not feel like one. **She is happy for her, plainly and completely**, and the thing she is holding does not make the happiness smaller. **Both at once, no tension between them, which is what six years of plain loyalty actually looks like.**

### Ch. 36 — Countdown

Year 5’s exam format and stakes laid out. **Proportionate, a checkpoint rather than a spectacle**, same principle as Book 3’s.

**[OPEN, carried from v1]** Year 5’s exam has no name and the combat reference has no Year 5 exam entry, only Flow Drills as coursework. **Wants naming before Ch. 39 through 41 are drafted.**

## ACT FOUR — The correction, Ch. 37-47, November into December

**Governing psychology:** the specific relief of a long-running wrongness finally being addressed, sitting next to a much larger wrongness that is not going to be. **Neither cancels the other and the book should end holding both.**

### Ch. 37 — Homecoming, Fifth Year

November. **Tier 3’s Homecoming fragment is the sharpest tonal marker in the whole bank against Book 1’s Tier 1 version.** Some of you do not have two homes to choose from anymore. This one still counts.

**Soren’s absence is a year old.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** B4.CH37’s arithmetic was off because the exchange was built for an even number. **A year on it has been rebuilt for the number they are**, and nobody rebuilt it deliberately, it just is that now. **One line and no weight.**

### Ch. 38 — The Ritual, Fifth Year

Hiopi ritual, and the cohort’s reactions now sorted by how much each of them has personally bled. **Vrassik is a winner watching, which is a different chapter than a competitor watching.**

### Ch. 39 — The Exam, Day One

Year 5’s assessment. **Weight and heat both real factors in a way no prior exam has had**, per the year’s curriculum.

**[CLAUDE, drafting]** The convergence pays off here without being named. Their machines are hotter and heavier than any exam has put them in, and their bodies have gone quietly automatic underneath, **and the exam is the only place in the book where both facts are operating at the same time on the same people.**

### Ch. 40 — The Exam, Day Two

**[CLAUDE]** Ilyen is scored as a Munti for the first time, six weeks into the role, and does badly, **and it is the best she has felt about an exam in five years.** Nobody comments on the discrepancy.

### Ch. 41 — The Exam, Final

Placements land, rank changes real.

**[CLAUDE]** Trav places genuinely well for the first time since Book 2. **Nothing in the book explains it and the reason is Ch. 13**, which is thirty chapters back and was not framed as an achievement when it happened.

### Ch. 42 — The Reposition

**The locked beat, and it gets real page time rather than a paragraph.**

Ilyen formally moves Reeps to Munti. **Framed the whole way through as a correction and never as a demotion**, per her sheet. She regains rank and gains real recognition once she is in a role where her precision and patience translate into visible success instead of quiet uncredited competence.

**Two Muntis on one team from here**, which is the setup for B6.CH20’s payoff.

*Psych: being good at something in public, for the first time, at sixteen, after four years of not being, is a genuinely large event and should be allowed to be one.*

**[CLAUDE, the four reasons, and this is the §1.3 constraint.]** She gives reasons if asked and there are four and they are all true: her precision, her patience, the Circles material’s overlap with diagnostic work, and the fact that the team needs a second Munti. **The doorway is not among them and never will be.**

**[CLAUDE, peak-line warning]** This is the book’s one genuinely joyful beat and it will invite a well-made closing sentence. **The peak line must be shorter and rougher than its setup.** If the last line of this chapter is the most grammatically complete one in it, flatten it.

### Ch. 43 — What Didn’t Change

**Immediately after, deliberately.**

The Cradle Circle thread does not resolve. The interference from Act 3 stands. **One kid got put in the right role by people who cared about her, inside a system that spent the same year quietly allocating resources away from her whole track, and the book puts those two facts next to each other and stops.**

**Rule 4 applies at maximum force. Nobody comments. Not Trav, not Reqa, not Osei, not narration.**

### Ch. 44 — Ilyen, Noticed

**Fourth of five tells, LOCKED.**

The chain escalates across the books: convenient scheduling (Book 2), watching the door before he arrives (Book 3), an unfinished sentence abandoned twice (Book 4), this, and Book 6’s reaction that outlasts the conversation.

**The fourth:** with real Munti capability for the first time, she starts keeping the specific parts his build eats, stocked ahead of need, **before he has asked for any of them.**

**Why this one and not a generic gesture.** It requires having tracked a pattern across months, which is the single most locked thing about her, the girl who keeps an unprompted private log of every AI-tutor correction the dorm gets purely because she likes knowing things precisely. **It is the same faculty that made her the cohort’s rune-patterning prodigy, pointed at one person.**

**And it is completely deniable.** A good Munti stocking for their team’s actual consumption is not a favor, it is the job, and she is newly excellent at the job. **Trav reads it as her being good at her new role. She is.**

**Craft note:** the reader should be able to work out that nobody else’s consumables are stocked this far ahead without a single line saying so. **Ch. 32 is that line’s absence made possible.**

Entirely platonic on the page, nothing physical, nothing named, and Trav never editorializes.

### Ch. 45 — Kinship

December, quiet, the team’s shared season. **Ilyen visibly better. Everything else visibly unchanged.**

**[MATCH]** Talia and Sana’s grip tape, Bruvald and Vekk’s flat hand. Two instances, unremarked, neither near the other.

### Ch. 46 — Loose Threads

**Deliberately calm, deliberately incomplete. The anger does not get discharged.**

**[CLAUDE, drafting, and the discipline is refusal.]** A drafting session will want to give the year’s anger an outlet. **There is not one and there is not going to be one until the civil war, twenty books out.** The chapter’s function is to demonstrate that.

### Ch. 47 — Bridge

Closes Year 5 and opens directly into Book 6, the transfer slot, and **the last full year the six of them have together.**

**[FORMULA, E→c.]**

**What Book 5 leaves.** In Trav: a body that has gone automatic underneath him, a year of showing up to a room he has no path to, and no knowledge whatever of what it produced. In Ilyen: a role that fits, four tiers of equipment to climb, and a habit of stocking one person’s parts. In Petra: the same thing she has been holding for four books, now heavier because the person is happy. In Talia: an answer that came from the work. In the cohort: a pattern they can all see and cannot touch.

**What Book 6 opens on. [CLAUDE, proposed override of B6.CH01.]** Not the transfer slot being announced. **Book 6 opens on Ilyen’s bench, stocked, in the first week of January, before anyone has asked for anything.** The announcement arrives in the chapter’s second movement.

